WEBVTT

00:39.039 --> 00:42.000
I'm sure you're aware Japan's ratio
of determining the cause of death

00:42.083 --> 00:43.710
is much lower than in other countries?

00:43.793 --> 00:44.627
Yes.

00:44.711 --> 00:46.045
To improve that,

00:46.129 --> 00:51.885
we're going to experimentally introduce
the American medical examiner system.

00:52.594 --> 00:54.262
Medical examiner?

00:54.345 --> 00:57.766
The biggest difference from Japan's system
is that medical examiners,

00:57.849 --> 01:01.519
or forensic pathologists,
have investigative authority.

01:16.868 --> 01:17.827
What?

01:18.369 --> 01:22.916
Does that mean doctors
can investigate cases like the police?

01:22.999 --> 01:25.627
No, not in exactly the same way,

01:25.710 --> 01:28.838
but they can conduct investigations
to determine the cause of death.

01:28.922 --> 01:31.674
They also have the authority
to determine the need for an autopsy.

01:33.802 --> 01:35.512
I see…

01:54.405 --> 01:55.323
So,

01:55.406 --> 01:57.826
we're bringing in Dr. Masumi Mizusawa,

01:57.909 --> 02:00.787
who has worked as a medical examiner
in the US for 15 years.

02:00.870 --> 02:02.163
And Ms. Kiryu,

02:02.247 --> 02:05.750
you'll be the director
of Medical Examiner Japan.

02:06.251 --> 02:07.252
Okay.

02:09.879 --> 02:10.797
What?

02:37.365 --> 02:39.242
Why am I being demoted?

02:39.325 --> 02:41.202
I mean, there's no way a policy change

02:41.286 --> 02:44.080
is going to increase the rate
of determining the cause of death.

02:44.956 --> 02:46.332
And you know,

02:46.416 --> 02:49.711
I became a bureaucrat
because I wanted to create systems

02:49.794 --> 02:52.755
that let everyone in this country
live their lives smiling.

02:53.339 --> 02:55.341
This topic is not even my specialty.

02:55.425 --> 02:56.968
I don't have the knowledge.

02:57.051 --> 02:59.178
And I'm supposed to be in charge on-site?

02:59.971 --> 03:01.556
There's no way I can do it.

03:06.269 --> 03:07.604
CENTER DIRECTOR
MAHO KIRYU

03:15.320 --> 03:16.988
Oh! Dr. Mizusawa?

03:17.071 --> 03:19.115
I'm Masumi Mizusawa.

03:19.198 --> 03:20.700
Call me Masumi.

03:20.783 --> 03:21.784
I'm Kiryu.

03:21.868 --> 03:23.870
-Pleasure to meet you.
-Same here.

03:24.495 --> 03:25.455
Huh?

03:26.164 --> 03:28.249
-Did you just come from the airport?
-Yes.

03:29.250 --> 03:31.377
Are you okay with your jet lag?

03:31.461 --> 03:33.254
Well… I don't know.

03:33.338 --> 03:37.926
You know, jet lag is like rigor mortis.

03:38.718 --> 03:40.595
Jet lag is like rigor mortis?

03:41.262 --> 03:42.555
What do you mean?

03:42.639 --> 03:43.514
Well…

03:44.015 --> 03:47.101
Jet lag doesn't happen
just because of time.

03:47.185 --> 03:50.313
It depends on light exposure
and the amount of sleep.

03:50.813 --> 03:53.566
Rigor mortis isn't determined
by time alone either.

03:53.650 --> 03:56.945
It changes based on temperature,
humidity, and posture.

03:57.028 --> 04:01.074
In other words, both depend
not just on time, but on conditions.

04:04.077 --> 04:05.119
Oh, okay.

04:06.287 --> 04:07.247
Right…

04:07.330 --> 04:08.164
Good.

04:10.750 --> 04:14.629
It finally starts tomorrow,
the medical examiner system.

04:17.423 --> 04:19.676
Ms. Dojima, should we call them?

04:19.759 --> 04:20.718
Call who?

04:20.802 --> 04:23.012
You know, the new system starting today,

04:23.096 --> 04:25.974
where we have to report
all abnormal deaths

04:26.057 --> 04:28.726
to that, uh, medical examiner.

04:28.810 --> 04:31.354
Oh, that ridiculous system.

04:31.437 --> 04:33.189
No need to call them.

04:33.273 --> 04:34.107
Yes, ma'am!

04:34.190 --> 04:36.943
At this hour, no one would even
answer the phone, right?

04:37.026 --> 04:38.111
Call them then.

04:38.194 --> 04:40.363
It will prove
that we tried to contact them.

04:40.446 --> 04:42.156
Yes, ma'am! I will call them!

04:44.993 --> 04:45.868
They answered!

04:45.952 --> 04:47.578
Sorry to keep you waiting.

04:47.662 --> 04:48.663
It's fine.

04:48.746 --> 04:51.791
Decomposition doesn't progress much
at this hour.

04:53.167 --> 04:54.002
What?

04:54.919 --> 04:55.837
Hello.

05:22.822 --> 05:24.240
Who are you? What are you--

05:24.324 --> 05:25.408
Hey, stop!

05:25.491 --> 05:27.827
-He's the medical examiner.
-Huh?

05:27.910 --> 05:30.163
What's that? I wasn't told about it.

05:30.246 --> 05:31.706
I'm the supervisor, Kiryu.

05:32.415 --> 05:34.625
With the new system, any unusual deaths

05:34.709 --> 05:37.253
must first be examined by
a forensic pathologist.

05:37.336 --> 05:38.880
The medical examiner will--

05:39.881 --> 05:40.798
Um…

05:44.510 --> 05:47.513
The deceased's mother
was the first to discover the body.

05:50.183 --> 05:52.226
His clothes are wet.

05:52.310 --> 05:54.312
Keitaro Aikawa, 17 years old.

05:54.395 --> 05:56.022
He didn't come home by dawn,

05:56.105 --> 05:59.567
so his mother got worried, searched
the neighborhood, and found him here.

05:59.650 --> 06:01.944
She called 119 and performed CPR.

06:02.028 --> 06:05.656
They determined he was clearly
already dead, so he wasn't transported.

06:05.740 --> 06:06.949
Found a smartphone.

06:07.033 --> 06:09.368
The pond is about 40 centimeters deep.

06:09.452 --> 06:10.661
-This is murder.
-Yes.

06:10.745 --> 06:13.664
There's no way someone drowns
in a pond this shallow.

06:14.707 --> 06:16.709
I'm sorry to bother you at a tough time.

06:16.793 --> 06:19.670
May I ask about the situation
when you found your son--

06:19.754 --> 06:21.672
Hey, don't… I'm sorry, Ma'am.

06:21.756 --> 06:22.840
Back off.

06:23.549 --> 06:24.634
Doctor.

06:24.717 --> 06:27.470
Could you stop interfering
in the investigation?

06:29.097 --> 06:30.389
I'm not interfering.

06:30.473 --> 06:31.307
What?

06:31.390 --> 06:32.850
You just did.

06:32.934 --> 06:35.937
This is a legitimate investigation
based on the authority

06:36.020 --> 06:38.314
granted to a medical examiner.

06:39.774 --> 06:41.109
Supervisor.

06:41.818 --> 06:43.653
Care to explain what's going on?

06:46.364 --> 06:49.075
In order to determine the cause of death,

06:49.158 --> 06:51.661
medical examiners
are granted investigative rights.

06:51.744 --> 06:53.329
Are you playing detective?

06:53.412 --> 06:56.582
Also, under this system,
all abnormal deaths must be evaluated,

06:56.666 --> 06:59.252
and the medical examiner
decides if an autopsy is needed.

06:59.335 --> 07:01.003
It's clearly a murder.

07:01.087 --> 07:02.755
It is, it is a murder!

07:02.839 --> 07:06.217
We still don't know
whether it's a homicide.

07:10.346 --> 07:11.305
You're in my way.

07:37.915 --> 07:40.376
Why is a bureaucrat the director here?

07:41.627 --> 07:42.462
What?

07:42.962 --> 07:44.964
Do you even have a medical license?

07:45.047 --> 07:46.340
Do you?

07:48.217 --> 07:52.889
No, I don't, but this is not only
a medical institution, it's also part of

07:52.972 --> 07:56.184
the country's policy
in determining the cause of death.

07:56.267 --> 07:59.353
There's a document from the Ministry
about autopsy regulations, right?

07:59.437 --> 08:00.354
Yes.

08:00.438 --> 08:03.149
Created by the department
overseeing the medical system.

08:03.733 --> 08:06.944
That document
is completely useless in the field.

08:07.445 --> 08:09.780
You don't say.

08:10.281 --> 08:11.574
Really?

08:12.909 --> 08:14.118
Of course, it is.

08:14.202 --> 08:17.163
It was made by bureaucrats
who don't understand the field.

08:27.131 --> 08:28.299
Let's begin.

09:00.248 --> 09:01.791
Are you okay?

09:01.874 --> 09:03.167
I'm totally fine.

09:04.710 --> 09:07.255
The cause of death is drowning.

09:07.338 --> 09:09.840
In a 40-centimeter-deep pond?

09:09.924 --> 09:12.301
-Yes.
-How could he have drowned there?

09:12.385 --> 09:15.263
So he drowned somewhere else
and was moved there?

09:15.346 --> 09:16.847
No, that doesn't add up.

09:16.931 --> 09:19.850
The plankton in the pond water
matches what was in his lungs.

09:19.934 --> 09:22.895
That means he was alive
when he entered the water.

09:22.979 --> 09:27.149
So he was assaulted, lost consciousness,
and then thrown into the pond?

09:27.233 --> 09:29.652
There were small abrasions on his palms.

09:29.735 --> 09:32.238
Possibly defensive injuries
from when he fell.

09:32.321 --> 09:35.491
That suggests he was conscious,
trying to defend himself.

09:35.575 --> 09:36.659
It doesn't add up.

09:36.742 --> 09:41.038
So then, he was pushed
into the pond while conscious?

09:41.122 --> 09:44.792
The pond is 40 centimeters deep.
He should have been able to stand up.

09:44.875 --> 09:45.918
It doesn't add up.

09:46.002 --> 09:49.171
Then maybe someone held his face down
while he tried to get up.

09:49.255 --> 09:53.551
That would leave marks on the head,
neck, and shoulders.

09:53.634 --> 09:55.011
It doesn't add up.

09:55.094 --> 09:58.431
So then how did he drown
in a 40-centimeter-deep pond?

09:58.514 --> 09:59.557
Who knows?

10:00.057 --> 10:01.601
I wonder.

10:02.351 --> 10:03.561
It's a mystery.

10:08.065 --> 10:09.066
Well, whatever.

10:10.026 --> 10:10.860
Then,

10:11.527 --> 10:13.613
what about signs of assault?

10:21.829 --> 10:27.251
There are injuries to the head and chest
caused by external force.

10:27.335 --> 10:30.588
First, on the head, the marks
are concentrated in two places.

10:30.671 --> 10:34.800
Likely left by a great external force,
such as from a weapon.

10:34.884 --> 10:37.845
As for the chest fractures,
there are three patterns.

10:37.928 --> 10:40.765
One was caused by CPR.

10:40.848 --> 10:44.268
There was no bleeding or vital reaction.

10:44.769 --> 10:46.562
In regard to this area,

10:46.646 --> 10:51.901
it shows a significant loss of blood,
and was very likely caused by assault.

10:51.984 --> 10:53.694
And the last one

10:53.778 --> 10:56.614
is one that can't be explained
by either of the other two.

10:58.282 --> 11:02.953
It does not show a strong vital reaction,
but there was something.

11:03.037 --> 11:06.874
This mark shows the slightest
amount of vital reaction.

11:06.957 --> 11:09.168
Is that the clue to
determining it was homicide?

11:09.251 --> 11:11.253
I don't know if it will or not.

11:11.337 --> 11:15.925
But it might be a piece of the puzzle
to solve the mystery.

11:18.928 --> 11:21.931
Anyway, solve it quickly.

11:22.014 --> 11:23.682
-Supervisor.
-Yes?

11:24.266 --> 11:26.477
Do your job and supervise.

11:36.112 --> 11:37.238
What's that?

11:38.030 --> 11:40.741
The content of the notebook by the pond.

11:40.825 --> 11:42.326
The phone next to it

11:42.410 --> 11:45.037
was the one that belonged to
Keitaro Aikawa.

11:45.121 --> 11:47.289
Then the phone in the pond…

11:47.373 --> 11:48.874
Still being verified.

11:51.460 --> 11:53.921
Does anyone know what this means?

11:54.422 --> 11:56.674
For now, it's a mystery.

11:56.757 --> 12:00.261
We identified the owner of the phone.

12:00.344 --> 12:02.721
-What?
-Ryoya Sakagami, 17 years old.

12:02.805 --> 12:04.181
One arrest for marijuana dealing.

12:04.265 --> 12:05.850
He's a member of a group

12:05.933 --> 12:08.936
based out of Club Heads
that engages in fraud and drug dealing.

12:16.735 --> 12:18.112
Why are you running away?

12:20.614 --> 12:21.490
Hey!

12:24.368 --> 12:27.037
Police! Nobody move!

12:34.712 --> 12:38.424
When was the last time
you saw Ryoya Sakagami?

12:39.216 --> 12:41.677
You were having him sell marijuana,
were you not?

12:41.760 --> 12:43.345
Don't know him.

12:43.429 --> 12:46.765
Cut the crap! Don't cover for a murderer!

12:47.266 --> 12:48.476
Murderer?

12:49.518 --> 12:50.394
Ryoya?

12:51.770 --> 12:52.938
Who did he kill?

12:53.022 --> 12:55.774
-Aikawa--
-A 17-year-old boy.

13:00.404 --> 13:01.447
You don't mean…

13:02.615 --> 13:03.824
Keitaro?

13:07.912 --> 13:09.830
How do you know that?

13:10.581 --> 13:12.625
Was he one of your group too?

13:13.918 --> 13:15.336
Any marijuana show up?

13:15.419 --> 13:16.670
Initially not,

13:16.754 --> 13:19.423
but when we retested
using liquid chromatography,

13:19.507 --> 13:23.260
marijuana was detected
in Keitaro Aikawa's blood and urine.

13:23.344 --> 13:24.386
I knew it.

13:24.470 --> 13:26.138
Huh? What does that mean?

13:26.222 --> 13:28.974
Keitaro Aikawa
was part of a marijuana trafficking group.

13:29.058 --> 13:32.603
He and one of the members, Ryoya Sakagami,
ran off with the marijuana.

13:32.686 --> 13:35.606
Then, they may have had
a falling out, and he was killed.

13:35.689 --> 13:39.652
We're currently tracking Ryoya Sakagami
as a key person of interest.

13:39.735 --> 13:42.363
I'm going to Keitaro Aikawa's home.

13:42.446 --> 13:44.865
-What's that?
-Some questions need answers.

13:44.949 --> 13:46.909
The numbers and arrows in the notebook,

13:46.992 --> 13:49.245
the weak vital signs
of the chest fracture,

13:49.328 --> 13:51.997
and why he drowned
in a 40-centimeter-deep pond.

13:52.081 --> 13:54.416
There may be pieces there
that solve these mysteries.

13:54.500 --> 13:55.834
Hey, Supervisor!

13:55.918 --> 13:59.255
Medical examiners are granted
investigative rights to gather info--

13:59.338 --> 14:00.965
-Hold on!
-Wait!

14:07.680 --> 14:08.931
SONGWRITING NOTES VOL 3

14:10.808 --> 14:12.226
SONGWRITING NOTES VOL 3

14:24.363 --> 14:26.073
DID THE SOUND GET WORSE??

14:30.035 --> 14:31.161
IT'S NOT FAIR

14:31.245 --> 14:32.371
GOD SAVE ME

14:33.873 --> 14:34.707
WHY?

14:34.790 --> 14:36.125
I'M SCARED… REALLY SCARED

14:39.128 --> 14:40.212
IT'S OVER

14:40.296 --> 14:41.755
I'M SO, SO SCARED

14:41.839 --> 14:43.132
SHIT SHIT SHIT

14:50.639 --> 14:51.682
Ms. Dojima.

14:51.765 --> 14:53.392
No marijuana was found.

14:56.186 --> 14:57.062
Let's go.

14:57.146 --> 14:58.022
Okay.

15:05.779 --> 15:09.033
Did Keitaro like music?

15:09.783 --> 15:11.911
If you're done, leave already.

15:25.466 --> 15:27.176
Man, I'm exhausted.

15:27.259 --> 15:29.803
Today felt insanely long. Way too long.

15:30.596 --> 15:32.806
Being demoted was humiliating enough,

15:32.890 --> 15:35.601
but going out into the field
made it even worse.

15:35.684 --> 15:37.394
I really can't do anything.

15:39.688 --> 15:42.483
I'm just there. Just watching.
But I'm the one in charge.

15:42.566 --> 15:44.485
I want to go back to my old job.

15:45.152 --> 15:47.780
There's no way
I'm stuck in the field forever, right?

15:49.031 --> 15:51.116
This is Medical Examiner Japan.

16:09.468 --> 16:12.596
He really liked music.

16:14.515 --> 16:15.933
It sounds silly, but…

16:17.351 --> 16:20.229
he used to say he wanted to be a musician…

16:22.231 --> 16:23.190
Even though

16:24.692 --> 16:27.736
becoming one
is harder than winning the lottery.

16:29.488 --> 16:30.406
But

16:31.448 --> 16:32.449
then…

16:34.618 --> 16:36.370
his hearing went bad.

16:39.206 --> 16:40.332
He said

16:41.709 --> 16:43.544
he was giving up on music.

16:45.337 --> 16:47.506
He had worked so hard at it,

16:48.173 --> 16:49.133
so I think

16:50.009 --> 16:51.677
it was a massive shock to him.

16:57.016 --> 16:58.142
Around that time,

16:59.977 --> 17:00.936
Ryoya…

17:02.938 --> 17:06.066
a delinquent he knew
from middle school, invited him.

17:07.109 --> 17:08.485
Because of him…

17:10.529 --> 17:12.156
Keitaro joined a bad crowd.

17:18.787 --> 17:19.997
Marijuana, huh?

17:21.081 --> 17:22.249
Maybe he…

17:26.003 --> 17:28.464
Maybe he was a good-for-nothing kid,

17:29.131 --> 17:30.507
but to me,

17:32.217 --> 17:33.969
he was my precious child.

17:38.515 --> 17:41.977
I want to know why this happened.

17:45.064 --> 17:46.982
I want to know what happened.

17:47.941 --> 17:50.110
The whole truth.

17:53.739 --> 17:54.865
Please.

17:56.658 --> 17:58.827
Find out the truth.

18:01.538 --> 18:03.165
If he was murdered…

18:05.918 --> 18:07.461
I will never forgive them.

18:10.464 --> 18:13.300
I have no future left, anyway.

18:14.551 --> 18:17.179
I'll live my life just to hate the killer.

18:46.125 --> 18:47.417
What are you doing?

18:52.506 --> 18:54.174
What? Sudoku?

19:34.923 --> 19:36.383
Hey, where are you going?

19:36.466 --> 19:38.302
-The pond.
-What?

19:38.385 --> 19:40.888
There might be a piece
I haven't found yet.

19:46.351 --> 19:47.603
What are you doing here?

19:48.061 --> 19:49.021
Investigating.

19:49.605 --> 19:51.231
Investigating, huh?

19:52.232 --> 19:54.109
Did the police find out anything?

19:58.780 --> 20:00.532
It wasn't on the day of the crime.

20:00.616 --> 20:03.118
He was seen with a man
thought to be Ryoya Sakagami.

20:03.202 --> 20:05.579
Standing opposite each other
across the pond.

20:05.662 --> 20:09.917
They were shouting, clapping,
and throwing stones into the water.

20:10.709 --> 20:13.545
Most likely, they were just high
and acting out.

20:26.308 --> 20:28.477
Oh, it's Ms. Dojima. Excuse me.

20:28.560 --> 20:29.686
Don't answer.

20:29.770 --> 20:31.647
-No, but--
-I said don't answer it.

20:35.150 --> 20:36.193
What?

21:19.778 --> 21:20.946
The pieces

21:21.780 --> 21:22.781
might be…

21:25.033 --> 21:26.326
coming together.

21:40.882 --> 21:41.758
Hey!

21:43.552 --> 21:44.594
Damn it!

21:46.305 --> 21:47.597
Let me go!

21:49.891 --> 21:51.059
Now what?

21:51.143 --> 21:53.729
-I would like you to check something.
-What?

21:53.812 --> 21:57.190
About the apps on Keitaro Aikawa's phone.

21:57.274 --> 21:58.400
Look, forget it.

21:58.483 --> 22:01.570
They caught Ryoya Sakagami,
and the interrogation is about to start.

22:01.653 --> 22:03.238
I'll be right there!

22:05.157 --> 22:07.534
-You're even coming to the interrogation?
-Yes.

22:07.617 --> 22:11.788
Based on the legitimate authority
granted to a medical examiner--

22:11.872 --> 22:13.332
Enough!

22:13.415 --> 22:14.875
Fine, I get it.

22:16.043 --> 22:19.546
You went to the pond on April 6th, right?

22:22.215 --> 22:24.051
What happened there?

22:29.222 --> 22:30.724
What did you do?

22:35.812 --> 22:37.314
I didn't go to the pond.

22:37.397 --> 22:40.442
Then why was your phone found in the pond?

22:47.991 --> 22:49.034
Excuse me.

22:49.117 --> 22:51.828
May I ask a question?

22:59.252 --> 23:00.378
Go ahead.

23:12.516 --> 23:14.101
Do you know

23:14.893 --> 23:15.936
what this is?

23:22.734 --> 23:23.985
You know

23:25.237 --> 23:26.988
what this means, don't you?

23:34.913 --> 23:35.747
Yes.

23:43.880 --> 23:44.923
And,

23:46.299 --> 23:51.138
there were three types of fractures
on Keitaro Aikawa's chest.

23:52.764 --> 23:53.807
The first

23:54.516 --> 23:55.976
was from being assaulted.

23:57.352 --> 23:58.520
The second

23:59.062 --> 24:02.107
was from his mother performing CPR.

24:05.193 --> 24:06.403
The third one…

24:08.405 --> 24:09.739
was from you, right?

24:20.834 --> 24:22.210
What happened?

24:30.302 --> 24:31.678
I'm sorry.

24:35.223 --> 24:36.266
I'm sorry.

24:37.893 --> 24:39.227
I'm sorry.

24:43.148 --> 24:44.566
I'm sorry.

24:49.654 --> 24:51.031
I'm sorry.

24:52.115 --> 24:53.450
I'm sorry.

25:01.666 --> 25:05.921
I will now explain
the cause of Keitaro's death.

25:06.963 --> 25:08.131
Please.

25:09.424 --> 25:10.800
We determined

25:10.884 --> 25:13.386
that the cause of death was drowning.

25:14.971 --> 25:18.350
First, around 5:00 p.m. the prior evening,

25:18.433 --> 25:20.977
he was struck in the head,
causing a hematoma,

25:21.061 --> 25:22.771
essentially a lump.

25:23.271 --> 25:27.442
Left alone, it was an injury
that would have healed naturally.

25:29.611 --> 25:32.822
After that, Keitaro came to this pond.

25:34.282 --> 25:36.409
This pond has a bridge spanning it,

25:36.493 --> 25:38.703
and the surrounding slopes
form a bowl shape,

25:38.787 --> 25:41.706
creating an environment
where sound echoes easily.

25:46.795 --> 25:48.463
MASUMI MIZUSAWA

25:51.758 --> 25:54.427
The sound bounces back
from various directions,

25:54.511 --> 25:57.973
making you lose sense
of where it's actually coming from.

25:58.556 --> 26:01.309
For Keitaro, who was hard of hearing
in his left ear,

26:01.393 --> 26:03.478
it was particularly confusing.

26:21.705 --> 26:23.748
Then, at around 10:00 p.m. that night,

26:23.832 --> 26:26.293
while Keitaro was standing
at the edge of the pond,

26:26.376 --> 26:28.420
a motorcycle horn honked.

26:29.796 --> 26:34.134
A horn is designed to resonate loudly
in a specific direction ahead.

26:34.676 --> 26:37.470
Keitaro got startled by it,

26:37.554 --> 26:40.432
feeling as though the sound source
was close to him.

26:40.515 --> 26:44.436
He instinctively tried to avoid
the loud noise from an unknown direction,

26:44.519 --> 26:46.896
and it is likely that he slipped.

26:46.980 --> 26:48.148
He fell into the pond

26:48.231 --> 26:50.775
and hit his head on the water's surface.

26:51.484 --> 26:52.652
At that moment,

26:52.736 --> 26:56.197
a second impact was delivered
to a brain that had already been damaged.

26:56.281 --> 27:00.577
This is known as Second Impact Syndrome.

27:01.578 --> 27:05.707
If the brain receives a second blow
before it has recovered from the first,

27:06.333 --> 27:11.671
even a minor force can amplify the damage
and lead to a fatal outcome.

27:12.255 --> 27:13.548
In Keitaro's case,

27:13.631 --> 27:17.218
he suffered a severe concussion
and lost consciousness.

27:17.844 --> 27:19.054
As a result,

27:19.929 --> 27:24.100
he drowned in a pond
only 40 centimeters deep.

27:26.061 --> 27:26.895
What?

27:28.813 --> 27:29.689
In other words,

27:31.316 --> 27:34.778
it was a tragic accident resulting
from a series of coincidences.

27:38.031 --> 27:39.115
An accident?

27:46.664 --> 27:49.209
If he hadn't been hit in the head,

27:49.292 --> 27:50.919
he wouldn't have died, right?

27:52.879 --> 27:55.256
If that horn hadn't been honked,

27:55.340 --> 27:56.883
he wouldn't have died.

27:58.009 --> 28:01.346
He died because someone did those things,
didn't he?

28:03.890 --> 28:06.643
And yet, you call it an accident.

28:09.938 --> 28:11.523
I won't accept that.

28:12.482 --> 28:14.025
I can't let it be.

28:15.527 --> 28:17.487
Keitaro was murdered.

28:19.406 --> 28:21.366
Wasn't it Ryoya who honked the horn?

28:21.449 --> 28:23.868
I know he rides a bike.

28:23.952 --> 28:26.204
That boy killed Keitaro, right?

28:26.287 --> 28:28.706
Arrest him. Aren't you the police?

28:28.790 --> 28:31.000
I understand how you feel.

28:31.084 --> 28:33.169
But based on the objective facts,

28:33.253 --> 28:36.548
the police are treating this case
as an accident.

28:36.631 --> 28:40.927
We have released the person of interest
after questioning.

28:41.010 --> 28:42.804
That's not right.

28:43.430 --> 28:45.390
Keitaro is dead,

28:45.473 --> 28:47.809
so why does he get to live a normal life?

28:50.311 --> 28:51.813
I understand how you feel,

28:52.313 --> 28:56.818
but these are the facts
derived from a forensic perspective.

28:58.027 --> 28:59.070
I told you

29:00.488 --> 29:02.866
I can't accept that.

29:11.332 --> 29:13.376
Shouldn't we tell her about it?

29:15.587 --> 29:16.671
That thing?

29:24.679 --> 29:25.889
Yes.

29:35.982 --> 29:39.986
My job is to convey the results
of the cause-of-death investigation.

29:41.321 --> 29:44.532
Anything else is not for me to say.

29:46.701 --> 29:47.660
However…

29:49.913 --> 29:52.165
you are the supervisor, Ms. Kiryu.

29:54.501 --> 29:57.086
If there is something else
you want to convey,

29:57.170 --> 30:00.048
please use your own judgment.

30:14.979 --> 30:16.397
But to me,

30:16.481 --> 30:18.733
he was my precious child.

30:18.817 --> 30:20.860
I want to know what happened.

30:21.444 --> 30:23.363
The whole truth.

30:34.791 --> 30:38.127
The one who honked the horn
was Ryoya Sakagami.

30:38.711 --> 30:39.504
Hey.

30:40.755 --> 30:42.131
What are you doing?

30:42.757 --> 30:44.884
It's the decision of the supervisor.

30:47.512 --> 30:48.930
Do what you want.

30:49.013 --> 30:51.266
The police won't take responsibility.

31:00.108 --> 31:01.276
Ms. Aikawa…

31:02.777 --> 31:06.906
Keitaro hadn't given up on his dream.

31:08.241 --> 31:09.909
How could you know that?

31:11.077 --> 31:14.831
It's the arrows and numbers
written in the notebook.

31:17.000 --> 31:19.544
Because Keitaro
could only hear out of one ear,

31:19.627 --> 31:23.965
the direction sound was coming from
differed from the actual source.

31:27.635 --> 31:30.138
He was using this pond
to confirm that deviation,

31:30.221 --> 31:32.682
and was trying to correct it himself.

31:35.226 --> 31:37.353
Close! You're almost there.

31:41.524 --> 31:43.484
The arrows are the direction of the sound,

31:43.568 --> 31:45.904
and the numbers represent
the angle of the shift.

31:47.947 --> 31:49.616
You're amazing, Keitaro.

31:49.699 --> 31:51.618
You're making progress.

31:52.577 --> 31:54.662
I shouldn't have invited you
into the gang.

31:55.622 --> 31:56.748
I'm really sorry.

32:00.293 --> 32:03.129
Do you think I can still be a musician?

32:05.548 --> 32:06.716
You will.

32:07.634 --> 32:08.885
If anyone can, it's you.

32:10.094 --> 32:11.846
So you have to get out of the gang.

32:14.974 --> 32:15.892
Right.

32:17.685 --> 32:18.603
But,

32:19.437 --> 32:21.648
my mom used to say all the time

32:21.731 --> 32:26.694
that becoming a musician
is harder than winning the lottery.

32:30.949 --> 32:34.494
And then, on that day, Keitaro was…

32:39.040 --> 32:39.874
I…

32:41.793 --> 32:43.211
want out.

32:43.962 --> 32:44.921
I don't want…

32:47.131 --> 32:48.675
to sell marijuana anymore.

32:59.102 --> 33:00.269
Come here!

33:01.396 --> 33:04.649
The trace amount of marijuana
detected in Keitaro

33:04.732 --> 33:07.402
was most likely from secondhand smoke.

33:20.957 --> 33:22.959
Can't you just let Keitaro go?

33:24.752 --> 33:26.504
I'll sell his share too.

33:27.797 --> 33:28.881
Just please

33:29.924 --> 33:31.217
let Keitaro out.

33:42.478 --> 33:43.438
Please!

33:44.397 --> 33:45.231
Hey.

33:45.982 --> 33:46.983
Hey!

33:47.066 --> 33:48.609
Stand up, asshole.

33:49.193 --> 33:50.611
Don't fuck with us!

33:52.864 --> 33:54.157
Please…

33:55.158 --> 33:56.034
Please!

33:56.117 --> 33:57.785
-You little shit…
-Please…

33:57.869 --> 33:58.745
Take that!

34:03.458 --> 34:04.375
Bastard!

34:04.459 --> 34:05.376
Please.

34:10.882 --> 34:12.133
Please.

34:12.216 --> 34:13.885
You little shit…

34:18.473 --> 34:19.557
Please.

34:23.269 --> 34:24.228
In exchange,

34:24.312 --> 34:27.023
you bring in three million yen
in one week.

34:38.242 --> 34:39.660
Thank you!

34:43.122 --> 34:44.540
Thank you!

34:49.003 --> 34:50.630
Thank you!

35:10.191 --> 35:11.442
Lame.

35:16.072 --> 35:17.448
He honked his horn

35:19.075 --> 35:21.702
because he wanted to tell Keitaro
the good news.

35:25.039 --> 35:28.126
He was just happy and honked it.

35:36.759 --> 35:37.718
Hey!

35:38.511 --> 35:40.304
Quit messing around.

35:48.646 --> 35:49.939
Keitaro!

35:50.022 --> 35:52.024
Hey! Keitaro? Hey!

35:52.108 --> 35:53.067
Hey!

35:56.487 --> 35:57.613
Keitaro!

35:57.697 --> 35:59.615
Keitaro!

35:59.699 --> 36:01.117
Keitaro!

36:02.160 --> 36:03.286
Keitaro…

36:04.162 --> 36:06.372
Keitaro!

36:06.455 --> 36:07.665
Keitaro…

36:19.594 --> 36:21.429
I thought he might have died

36:22.972 --> 36:24.932
because of the CPR I gave him.

36:28.477 --> 36:29.896
I got scared and ran away.

36:35.776 --> 36:36.903
I'm sorry.

36:48.122 --> 36:50.082
If Ryoya had called an ambulance,

36:51.334 --> 36:52.752
would he have lived?

36:55.004 --> 36:55.922
No.

36:58.049 --> 36:59.592
Even if he had called one,

37:00.593 --> 37:02.720
the result would have been the same.

37:05.473 --> 37:08.601
There was a fracture
on Keitaro's chest that showed

37:08.684 --> 37:11.395
a very slight vital reaction.

37:12.521 --> 37:13.481
That was

37:14.649 --> 37:17.026
from the CPR performed

37:18.027 --> 37:19.987
by Ryoya Sakagami.

37:21.864 --> 37:25.117
The fact that the vital reaction
was so minimal

37:25.201 --> 37:28.704
proves that the CPR
was performed immediately after death.

37:28.788 --> 37:31.624
In other words, it means that Keitaro

37:33.334 --> 37:35.294
had already passed away.

37:48.432 --> 37:49.475
Ms. Aikawa.

37:52.395 --> 37:53.938
CPR…

37:56.190 --> 37:57.650
was performed twice.

38:00.695 --> 38:06.200
The two sets of marks
on Keitaro's chest were both

38:07.285 --> 38:08.577
left by people

38:09.996 --> 38:13.791
who desperately wanted him to live.

38:18.838 --> 38:20.131
Keitaro…

38:20.798 --> 38:22.133
Keitaro!

38:22.633 --> 38:23.843
Please!

38:24.593 --> 38:25.594
Keitaro!

38:26.262 --> 38:27.555
Stay with me, Keitaro!

38:27.638 --> 38:28.889
Keitaro!

38:29.682 --> 38:30.599
Keitaro!

38:30.683 --> 38:32.059
Keitaro!

38:32.143 --> 38:33.811
Please!

38:33.894 --> 38:35.354
Keitaro!

38:35.438 --> 38:36.522
Keitaro!

38:36.605 --> 38:37.690
Keitaro!

38:37.773 --> 38:39.191
Keitaro!

38:40.067 --> 38:41.569
Keitaro!

38:41.652 --> 38:42.945
Keitaro!

38:49.952 --> 38:51.037
So…

38:56.125 --> 38:58.169
you're telling me to forgive him?

39:05.051 --> 39:06.844
Don't make it sound so simple.

40:28.801 --> 40:30.845
Dying like that…

40:32.930 --> 40:35.808
is harder than winning
the lottery, isn't it?

40:45.484 --> 40:46.527
I'm sorry.

40:55.327 --> 40:56.704
I'm sorry.

40:58.414 --> 40:59.582
I'm sorry.

41:00.374 --> 41:01.417
I'm sorry.

41:05.379 --> 41:06.547
I'm sorry.

41:10.176 --> 41:11.302
I'm sorry.

41:18.392 --> 41:19.643
I'm sorry…

41:49.548 --> 41:52.259
Regarding the pickup
of Keitaro Aikawa's body,

41:52.343 --> 41:55.721
the funeral director should be here
in about 20 minutes.

41:55.804 --> 41:57.681
Okay. Thank you.

42:06.649 --> 42:09.401
Thank you for today.

42:14.448 --> 42:15.282
But,

42:17.117 --> 42:18.702
I think my decision today

42:20.204 --> 42:21.539
was one step away

42:23.290 --> 42:27.211
from rubbing the bereaved mother
the wrong way.

42:39.557 --> 42:40.724
In America,

42:41.725 --> 42:44.770
they call the deceased a "Loved One."

42:48.023 --> 42:49.066
Loved One?

42:56.949 --> 42:58.492
Does that mean someone…

43:00.494 --> 43:02.288
who was important

43:04.373 --> 43:06.041
to someone else?

43:09.378 --> 43:10.713
The term…

43:13.591 --> 43:14.800
is hard to translate.

43:16.594 --> 43:17.595
What?

43:20.347 --> 43:22.474
It's a word only used at the scene,

43:23.934 --> 43:26.520
and also a word only for oneself.

43:28.897 --> 43:31.317
A word only for oneself?

43:33.152 --> 43:34.153
Yes.

43:37.615 --> 43:39.366
Only you can decide

43:40.534 --> 43:42.911
what it means.

43:44.913 --> 43:46.999
Your decision today

43:47.750 --> 43:53.172
was your own way
of facing a Loved One, wasn't it?

44:36.131 --> 44:37.466
The death was caused by a fall.

44:37.549 --> 44:38.759
Is it fun?

44:38.842 --> 44:41.261
Talking about someone's death like that?

44:41.345 --> 44:43.889
If I had known it would be
the last time, I--

44:43.972 --> 44:45.474
It's not over yet.

44:45.557 --> 44:47.017
You're a forensic pathologist.

44:47.101 --> 44:49.937
There may still be a piece
we haven't found yet.

44:50.020 --> 44:50.896
Subtitle translation by: Maslin F.
